contrib/pg_prosupport/README.bounded_numeric_agg

Bounded numeric aggregates
==========================

This file documents the mechanism behind bounded_numeric_sum,
bounded_numeric_sum_mul, bounded_numeric_avg and bounded_numeric_avg_mul --
the aggregates pg_prosupport substitutes for sum(numeric)/avg(numeric)
through agg_simplify_hook (see the top-level README for how and when the
substitution fires; this file is only about what the substituted aggregate
does differently and why that is faster). It exists because "why is this
faster" has more than one answer, and none of them is "it's written in C" --
sum(numeric) is already written in C. The wins are structural, and each one
is independent of the others: removing any single one of them gives some of
the benefit back.

The problem with sum(numeric)
------------------------------

sum(numeric) and avg(numeric) share NumericAggState (numeric.c) as their
transition state. It holds an arbitrary-precision running total, accumulated
digit array by digit array through accum_sum_add(), because that is what a
NUMERIC column's contract requires in general: unbounded precision, exact
decimal arithmetic, no assumption about how many digits a value has.

Two costs follow directly from that generality, and both are paid on every
single input row, not just once per group or once per query:

  - accum_sum_add() walks the addend's digit array (base NBASE = 10000, four
    decimal digits per NumericDigit) and does carry-propagating addition into
    a bank of int32 accumulators, one per digit position -- because the
    result's width is not known ahead of time, the state has to be prepared
    to hold as many digits as the widest input plus overflow room seen so
    far. This is a small loop, but it runs on every row, and it is a loop
    over a variable-length array rather than one machine instruction.

  - The state itself is not a fixed-size struct. NumericAggState's sumX field
    (a NumericSumAccum) grows its digit buffer with repalloc() as wider
    values arrive, and every input Numeric that reaches do_numeric_accum()
    was itself built by detoasting and, for anything that went through
    arithmetic upstream, by a separate palloc'd NumericVar with its own
    digit buffer. None of this is a single cache-line object: it is a
    struct holding pointers to separately allocated arrays, and the
    allocator, not just the CPU, is on the hot path.

hash_agg_entry_size() -- what plans the spill point of a HashAggregate --
takes the size of one group's transition state from aggtransspace in
pg_prosupport--1.0.sql. For sum(numeric)/avg(numeric) that number describes a
struct whose real footprint, digit buffers included, is larger and less
predictable than the declared value; the planner's estimate of how many
groups fit in work_mem is accordingly optimistic, and a query that looked
like it would stay in memory spills.

The state: one cache line, no pointers
----------------------------------------

NasAggState (numeric_agg.c) replaces all of that with:

    typedef struct NasAggState
    {
        INT128      sumX;         /* sum of mantissas at scale "scale" */
        int64       N;
        int64       NaNcount;
        int64       pInfcount;
        int64       nInfcount;
        int32       scale;
    } NasAggState;

Fifty-six bytes (checked by a StaticAssertDecl, so a change here that isn't
matched in pg_prosupport--1.0.sql's sspace fails the build, not silently
skews planning), no field that is a pointer to something else. Accumulating
a value is one INT128 addition. There is no repalloc, no digit array, no
detoast-then-reparse of an intermediate NumericVar for the running total --
the total lives entirely inside the struct itself, and the struct never
grows.

This is what aggtransspace being accurate actually buys: hash_agg_entry_size()
now reflects the true memory cost of a group, so the spill point the planner
computes is the spill point that happens, and a HashAggregate with a lot of
groups keeps three to four times as many of them in the same work_mem before
falling back to disk (measured, not estimated -- see the extension's own
README.md and bench/ for the numbers on real data).

The other side of "no pointers" is cache behaviour under GROUP BY. A
HashAggregate's entries are read and written in whatever order the hash
table visits them, which is not the order rows arrived in; a state that is
one contiguous 56-byte object stays in one cache line, while a
NumericAggState's digit buffers are separate allocations the CPU has to chase
a pointer to reach, once per group per row. The size difference and the
pointer-chasing difference are separate effects, and the second one does not
show up in aggtransspace at all -- it shows up as cache misses, and it is why
the measured win is larger than the raw byte-count difference alone would
predict.

Where the mantissa comes from: no intermediate Numeric, no NumericVar either
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Accumulating an INT128 requires turning the input Numeric into "the value,
shifted by scale decimal places, as an integer" -- the mantissa. The obvious
way to get that is arithmetic: multiply the numeric by 10^scale using the
existing numeric_mul() machinery, then convert the (now integral) result to
an integer. That would still cost a full NumericVar allocation and a real
multiplication per row, defeating the point. A gentler-looking shortcut --
unpack the Numeric into a NumericVar via init_var_from_num() and read its
digits from there -- still copies nothing but still pays for the unpacking
step and its bookkeeping.

pps_get_bounded_int128() (numeric_agg.c) does neither. It reads straight off
the Numeric's own packed on-disk representation -- the digit array inside
the varlena's union NumericChoice, reached through this module's own
PPS_NDIGITS()/PPS_WEIGHT()/PPS_DIGITS() macros, which do not copy or
allocate, they just index into bytes that are already in memory -- and runs
a Horner-scheme reduction directly over those base-10000 digits into a
native INT128:

    m = 0;
    for each digit d in the value, most significant first:
        m = m * NBASE + d;

with one correction for the difference between the value's own stored scale
and the target scale (a single multiply, or an exact-division check by a
power of ten below NBASE for the common case where the value's digits run
past the target scale -- since the two scales can only ever differ by fewer
than four decimal digits, the rest of the alignment falls out of the Horner
loop itself). This is arithmetic on machine integers using the digits
PostgreSQL already has in hand, not a second numeric value built and thrown
away, and not even a NumericVar's worth of bookkeeping around them.

The precision bound (see below) exists precisely so this loop cannot
overflow: every partial product stays under 10^28, comfortably inside INT128
regardless of how many digits are left to fold in.

Reconstruction happens once per group, not once per row
------------------------------------------------------------

The inverse operation -- turning the accumulated INT128 mantissa back into a
NUMERIC, in pps_bounded_sum_final() -- is comparatively expensive: it splits
the mantissa into three 18-digit chunks and rebuilds the value through three
calls to int64_div_fast_to_numeric() plus two numeric_add_opt_error() calls,
because that is the only way to reintroduce the correct weight/dscale
without a bespoke 128-bit-aware division routine. (An early version of this
code tried to skip that by just relabelling an integer-valued NumericVar's
dscale field; dscale does not move the decimal point, so that produced sums
100x too large whenever scale was 2 -- caught by pg_prosupport's own
differential regression tests against stock sum(), not by anything in core's
suite, which is why those tests matter and are worth keeping in sync.)

The reason this is not a problem is arithmetic, not cleverness: the final
function runs once per group (once, full stop, for a plain scalar
aggregate), while the accumulator above runs once per row. A HashAggregate
over a million rows and ten thousand groups pays the O(1) per-row mantissa
extraction a million times and the three-chunk reconstruction ten thousand
times. Moving cost from the row-count side of that ratio to the group-count
side is the same trade sum(int8) already makes by accumulating into a bare
int128 and only building a NumericVar in its own finalfn.

Folding a product: no intermediate Numeric at all
-----------------------------------------------------

sum(a * b) ordinarily calls numeric_mul() once per row: that allocates a
result NumericVar, packs it into a Numeric, and hands it to the transition
function, which promptly unpacks it again to accumulate. Nothing about the
packed product survives past that one transition call, so the packing was
pure overhead.

bounded_numeric_sum_mul (sfunc pps_bounded_accum_mul) skips building the
product Numeric entirely: it extracts each factor's mantissa the same way
pps_get_bounded_int128() does, as a plain int64 this time (each factor is
bounded at 18 digits so its mantissa fits one), and accumulates their
product directly into the INT128 state with int128_add_int64_mul_int64() --
the same primitive int8_avg's own transition function uses for exactly this
shape. One multiply-accumulate against one alloc-multiply-pack-unpack: on
the measurements in the extension's README, this is what turns an 8%
end-to-end gain from scale specialisation alone into roughly 32% once the
product is folded in too.

Zero-cost dispatch: the decision is made once, at plan time
-----------------------------------------------------------

None of the above would be free if it were decided per row. It is not: the
substitution -- which aggregate to run at all -- happens once, while the
query is being planned, in eval_const_expressions_mutator()'s T_Aggref case
(patched into clauses.c; see the top-level README). agg_simplify_hook calls
pg_prosupport's own hook, which calls pps_simplify_bounded_numeric_agg()
(numeric_support.c): it looks at the Aggref once, proves the argument's
precision and scale statically -- from a column's typmod, from a cast, CASE
or domain, or by walking an arithmetic expression via pps_derive_bounds() --
and either returns a replacement Aggref naming one of the four aggregates
above or leaves the original alone. By the time the plan is executed, there
is no branch, no runtime type check, no "is this the kind of numeric I can
specialise" test anywhere in the per-row path -- the executor calls
whichever transition function the plan says to call, exactly as it would for
any other aggregate. The cost of deciding is paid once per query (in
practice, once per plan, since a generic plan is not replanned), not once
per row.

Unlike the state and the mantissa arithmetic described above, this decision
step does have a configuration to consult and a chain to fall through to --
pg_prosupport.bounded_numeric_agg, and the possibility that some other
loaded module's hook runs next -- because unlike "is this loop safe from
overflow", "should this rewrite even be attempted this session" is a
question the state itself cannot answer. See the top-level README for that
part.

Why 1 <= p <= 28 is safe, and why it is checked once
--------------------------------------------------------

The bound comes from the accumulator, not from the mantissa extraction: at
p = 28 a mantissa is below 10^28 ~ 2^93, so INT128's headroom above that
(2^127) allows roughly 2^34, about seventeen billion, further additions
before the accumulator itself could overflow -- the same argument sum(int8)
relies on when it accumulates into int128 unchecked. This is proven once,
statically, from the argument's typmod (or from the arithmetic expression it
is derived through), at the moment the Aggref is substituted -- not
re-checked on every row the way a runtime overflow guard would have to be.
The per-row cost of that safety is therefore zero; what each row does pay is
the exact-fit check inside pps_get_bounded_int128() (does this particular
value's digits actually match the scale it was specialised on), which is not
a safety margin but a promise-keeping check: a numeric(p,s) column is
supposed to guarantee every value fits, but a value can still arrive from an
FDW, or out of a cast that bypassed typmod enforcement, without honouring
it. When that happens the aggregate raises an ERROR rather than silently
producing a value 10^k off -- correctness is still checked per row, but it
is a cheap comparison against already-unpacked digits, not a fallback to
arbitrary-precision arithmetic.
